# Make the seed-1 Lizardfolk monk with EMPTY HANDS and SAVE him, so he can be
# frozen.
#
#   INCURSION_OPTIONS=tools/fixtures/options-2026-08-18.dat \
#       tools/headless.sh tools/keys/lizardfolk-monk-barehand-save.keys 1
#
# WHY A SECOND MONK. tools/fixtures/chars/lizardfolk-monk-seed1.sav holds this
# same character exactly as chargen handed him over, with a weapon in his
# hands. A fixture is frozen and a different character is a new fixture, never
# an edit (tools/fixtures/README.md), so "the same monk, bare-handed" has to be
# its own file. It is worth its own file because src/Sheet.cpp:162 hides the
# whole Brawl block while SL_WEAPON holds a weapon: every check that measures
# bare-handed fighting must empty the hands first, and until this fixture
# existed each of them emptied the hands for itself, in its own chargen prefix,
# against whatever weapon that day's module happened to hand the character.
# That is how tools/keys/monk-tempest.keys went red -- see bd inc-sls0.
#
# THE CHARGEN IS A COPY of tools/keys/lizardfolk-monk-save.keys, letter for
# letter, down to the dungeon. The two fixtures are then the SAME character and
# the only difference between them is the hands, which is the only way the
# second one is worth having. Do not "improve" it here; make a third script.
#
# THE SETTINGS ARE THE CONSUMER'S. tools/check_two_fist_feats_live.sh runs on
# tools/fixtures/options-2026-08-18.dat, so this fixture is generated on that
# file rather than on the 2026-08-22 one the first fixture used. A fixture is
# only meaningful with the settings that made it, and matching the check that
# loads it is what keeps the two halves in step.
#
# IT IS DROPPED, NOT STOWED, AND THAT IS NOT A SHORTCUT. Stowing is what
# tools/keys/monk-brawl-speed.keys does, and it is refused here: the
# quarterstaff this character now starts with is bigger than his medium
# backpack, so 's' answers "The quarterstaff is too large to fit in the medium
# backpack", the hand stays full, and nothing downstream notices. Dropping
# cannot be refused for size. The staff lands on the floor of the entry chamber
# and stays in the save as an "On The Ground" line, which is also the reader's
# evidence that the hands were emptied on purpose.
#
# ONE KEY EMPTIES BOTH HANDS, because the quarterstaff is two-handed and fills
# Ready Hand and Weapon Hand at once. Slot [b] is named rather than [a] because
# SL_WEAPON is the slot src/Sheet.cpp:162 actually tests.
#
# ESC b is the System Menu's [b] Save and Continue (src/Player.cpp:843), and it
# is the only save the game offers that does not also end the session. READ THE
# WARNING IN tools/keys/save-continue.keys BEFORE MOVING IT: ESC opens that menu
# rather than cancelling anything, [a] is Save and Quit, and a stray ESC
# therefore ends the run and still exits 0. The @expect below is the guard
# against exactly that -- it stops the run (exit 6) if the screen is not the
# System Menu, so a drifted key count can never press [a] by accident.

a       # Initial Choices: [a] Create a New Character
n       # Alter the character generation options? -- no
y       # Your options will place the game in Explore Mode. Continue? -- yes
h       # Race:     [h] Lizardfolk
a       # Subrace:  [a] Lizardfolk (Standard)
e       # Class:    [e] Monk
a       # Attributes: the first of the five offered sets
c       # Alignment: [c] Neutral
a       # First feat
a       # Second feat

# Skill Manager, twice. Three RIGHTs per skill spends everything available
# without needing to know how many ranks this character has.
RIGHT*3 DOWN RIGHT*3 DOWN RIGHT*3 DOWN RIGHT*3 DOWN RIGHT*3 DOWN RIGHT*3 DOWN
RIGHT*3 DOWN RIGHT*3 DOWN RIGHT*3 DOWN RIGHT*3 DOWN RIGHT*3 DOWN RIGHT*3 DOWN
ENTER y

y       # Do you wish to worship <god>? -- yes

RIGHT*3 DOWN RIGHT*3 DOWN RIGHT*3 DOWN RIGHT*3 DOWN RIGHT*3 DOWN RIGHT*3 DOWN
RIGHT*3 DOWN RIGHT*3 DOWN RIGHT*3 DOWN RIGHT*3 DOWN RIGHT*3 DOWN RIGHT*3 DOWN
ENTER y

# In the dungeon now. Three keys settle the opening: the room description
# wants dismissing, then two messages want acknowledging. A fourth ESC here
# opens the System Menu instead and every later key lands in it.
ESC ENTER SPACE

@dump:arrival

# Empty the hands. No confirmation is expected: OPT_WARN_DROP is off in these
# settings, so 'd' drops at once. The @expect is the guard that says so out
# loud -- if the option is ever turned on, a "Confirm drop" prompt would eat
# the ESC below and this run would stop here (exit 6) instead of freezing a
# character who is still holding his staff.
i                       # Inventory Mode
b                       # Weapon Hand -- the slot src/Sheet.cpp:162 tests
d                       # Drop. Stowing is refused for size; see the header.
@expect "Weapon Hand  :Empty"
@dump:hands-empty
ESC                     # leave Inventory Mode, back to play

ESC                     # System Menu -- and NOT a cancel; see the header
@expect "System Menu"   # refuse to press a letter into any other screen
b                       # [b] Save and Continue

# The save is not written by the keypress. Case 2 of the System Menu sets the
# autosave flag and Timeout = 1 (src/Player.cpp:851-853); Game::Play writes the
# file at the top of the next pass of its loop (src/Main.cpp:266-275), which is
# before it asks for another key. This dump therefore fires AFTER the file is
# on disk, and photographs the engine's own "Autosave... done." line saying so.
# That line is the check's oracle that a fixture was written at all.
@dump:saved

# THERE IS NO @quit HERE, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE. @quit becomes
# KY_CMD_QUICK_QUIT (src/Wposix.cpp:1657-1664), which is Save and Quit: it
# would write the save a SECOND time, three turns later than the one above,
# and shuffle the first copy off to <name>.sav.backup. The fixture would then
# be a file no line of this script asked for. Letting the key script run out
# instead ends the session with exactly one save, made at the point this
# script names.
